WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.370
Welcome to "Ear Biscuits", I'm Rhett.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.430
And I'm Link.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:10.020
This week at the
roundtable of dim lighting,

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.190
I'm gonna tell you a story.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:13.550
I wanna take you--
Story time.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:18.260
A true story, I wanna
take you on a journey.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.170
A chain of events that began

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:24.300
in the wake of episode before last,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:28.080
Our First Memories episode.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:28.930
Yep.

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:30.300
You remember that, Rhett?

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.700
I've already forgotten it.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:37.160
Well, you know the story I'm gonna tell.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.920
It's a story of confrontation, of pain,

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.250
forgiveness and the power of words.

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.950
You really set it up.

00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:52.470
And it's also the story,
specifically, of one word.

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:56.833
One very special word.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.780
It's been a wild ride over
the last couple of weeks

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:02.007
and I just got to a
point where I was like,

00:01:02.007 --> 00:01:05.630
"I have to share what has happened."

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.500
Maybe this won't be as sensational

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.497
or as groundbreaking to
anybody else but for me--

00:01:09.497 --> 00:01:11.418
You talking about it in that tone,

00:01:11.418 --> 00:01:14.020
I think it will be
sensational just because--

00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:16.173
I'm sensationalizing it but it--

00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:17.797
I don't know if it requires that.

00:01:17.797 --> 00:01:19.590
Yeah, I don't think it requires that.

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:21.530
I will say that it's just been something

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:23.250
that's been deeply meaningful to me

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.450
and I think it's shaped
who I am as a person.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.745
It's directly related to this podcast.

00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:28.640
Wow.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.580
And--

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:30.870
Does it have to be that deep

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.960
in order for it to be
worthy of the podcast?

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:33.820
Okay, that's fair.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:38.310
Well, it has shaped how I conduct myself

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.090
on this podcast moving forward.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.300
It's a wild ride, I'm--

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:46.197
Well, I'm looking forward to that, Link.

00:01:46.197 --> 00:01:50.670
But it has an unexpected
double happy ending.

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:54.340
By show of hands, who's ready
for a double happy ending?

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.110
I don't know if I can handle that.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.340
You shouldn't have
actually raised your hands

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:00.773
because that's kinda
weird, kind of awkward.

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.380
Before we get into Link's story--

00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.420
Do you want a double happy ending today?

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.380
Nope, I don't believe in happy endings.

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.180
I don't go to those kinds of places.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.010
You talking about like fairy tales?

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.190
The first thing I wanna
do is I wanna let you know

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.690
that the Mythical Kitchen
has launched its own podcast

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.310
called "A Hot Dog Is a Sandwich"

00:02:26.310 --> 00:02:29.920
and it is a weekly podcast
that comes out every Wednesday.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.680
I'm so excited for 'em.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.440
Where Josh and Nicole will debate

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.350
a controversial food topic.

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.920
Now, one of the things that
we noticed about Josh early on

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.470
when he started working here was,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:43.170
I mean, you've seen him demonstrate this

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:45.640
on Good Mythical Morning
and the Mythical Kitchen.

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.390
Dude is a food encyclopedia

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.860
but he's not just an encyclopedia

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.710
because an encyclopedia
presents just facts.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:58.530
No, Josh goes above facts
and starts inserting his own

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:02.730
very well-formed, well-reasoned
opinions about food,

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:04.237
which is a subjective thing to begin with.

00:03:04.237 --> 00:03:06.360
I mean we're having like a party.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:08.860
Like a Mythical Entertainment party.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:09.693
People gather around it.

00:03:09.693 --> 00:03:13.210
I see that there was like a
crowd of people over there

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.620
and they were all around
Josh just grilling him

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.350
about food questions
and so I went over there

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:22.900
and just got a kick out of
just listening in on it.

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.890
The podcast wasn't our
idea but I'm very excited

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:29.830
that now, you have the opportunity

00:03:29.830 --> 00:03:31.420
to have the same experience that we have

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:34.250
at our Mythical get-togethers.

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:35.083
And then--

00:03:35.083 --> 00:03:36.161
Did I say what the first episode was?

00:03:36.161 --> 00:03:38.990
No, Nicole is there to--

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:39.823
Balance him.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.780
I don't know exactly what she,

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.204
I hope balance him.

00:03:45.204 --> 00:03:46.560
Keep him in check.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:47.740
First episode, they're arguing

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:51.290
about whether or not
pineapple belongs on pizza,

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.730
which is a controversial thing.

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.260
It's already out wherever you listen to,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:57.920
if you're listening to this,
you can also listen to that.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:03:59.380
And then a portion of the podcast

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.100
and video form will come out the day after

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.770
on the Mythical Kitchen YouTube
channel, so check it out.

00:04:03.770 --> 00:04:04.920
Check it out, y'all.

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:05.854
Check that out.

00:04:05.854 --> 00:04:08.250
I have a little something that
happened to me this morning

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.130
that I think I need to just get out there.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:11.963
Sensationalize it.

00:04:11.963 --> 00:04:13.350
Like, did it change your life?

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:14.200
I don't think

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.240
it need to be sensationalized.
Is it a story of pain,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.010
forgiveness and the power of words?

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:17.983
No, it's just a fun little story, Link.

00:04:17.983 --> 00:04:19.700
All right, okay.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.853
The bar can be low.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:25.190
First of all, my wife
is out of town, okay?

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:26.940
She's hanging out with friends.

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:29.480
Explains why you're dressed
like a children's entertainer.

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:30.330
Right, exactly.

00:04:31.616 --> 00:04:33.850
Well, first of all, my
wife doesn't dress me

00:04:33.850 --> 00:04:35.237
when she's there.
Yes, she does.

00:04:35.237 --> 00:04:37.683
She doesn't lay my clothes out anymore.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:41.540
It is funny, because this morning,

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.910
Christy happened to be
standing in the closet

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:46.130
when I was also in the
closet picking out my clothes

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.057
and I was like, "Christy,
I want you to pick out

00:04:48.057 --> 00:04:49.457
"what I'm gonna wear today."

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:52.472
That's weird, that's weird.

00:04:52.472 --> 00:04:53.305
Did she?

00:04:53.305 --> 00:04:54.150
And this is it?

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:56.970
No, once I got here,
Kiko said they wanted me

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.840
to wear merch so I had to change clothes.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:04:59.673
Oh, okay.

00:04:59.673 --> 00:05:00.506
This is not

00:05:00.506 --> 00:05:02.060
what Christy picked.
What about the outerwear?

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:02.993
I changed the jacket
'cause it didn't match.

00:05:02.993 --> 00:05:04.420
What about the jeans?

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.720
You had a story to tell.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.880
So she's out of town so,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.403
the McLaughlin household
is hanging on by thread.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:16.030
The kids, I think they're being fed.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.630
I'm pretty sure, I have
seen some like crumbs

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:20.313
in the kitchen.

00:05:22.729 --> 00:05:25.630
I doubt that they're bathing

00:05:25.630 --> 00:05:27.280
but that's not my responsibility.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.340
So, of course--

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:32.479
My wife bathed me.

00:05:32.479 --> 00:05:35.383
I have to take them to school.

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.053
And not every day.

00:05:38.053 --> 00:05:39.550
I don't have to pick them up every day

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:40.870
but I have to take them
to school every day

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:42.290
while she's gone.

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:45.320
And first of all, just
shout out to the parents

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.690
who have to take their kids to school

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.030
and pick them up and take them everywhere.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:52.220
I think you should unionize

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:56.070
because just after doing this
just for a couple of days,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:58.610
which I'm not saying I've
never taken my kids to school

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.840
but like, when my wife is not there

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:03.850
and so I've got to kind of be responsible

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:05.890
for getting them up and
making sure they're ready

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:07.480
and everybody getting
ready to go out the door

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:08.913
and then also take them.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:12.530
And then, what my wife has to do

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:14.280
is she's got to pick them
up but then also take them

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.260
to all the other crap that they got to do

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.310
and I just really feel like
there should be a union.

00:06:19.310 --> 00:06:21.430
I mean if the taxi drivers can unionize,

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:23.720
why can't the parental drivers unionize?

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.640
Because they got rights.

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:27.290
They have to have some rights like--

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:28.123
Like what?

00:06:28.123 --> 00:06:30.240
Like the right to play the
music that they wanna play,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:31.700
not that the kids wanna play.

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:32.533
Oh.

00:06:32.533 --> 00:06:36.110
The right to keep the kids
from eating crumby foods

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:37.760
in the back of the car.

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:40.330
The right to keep the kids from leaving

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:43.743
their freaking belongings in the car.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:48.630
Okay.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:50.480
Those kinds of things.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:53.660
I think it's also the right to insist

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:57.650
that the kids respond when
the parent asks a question.

00:06:57.650 --> 00:06:58.620
How was your day?

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:02.030
Yeah, you're actually gonna
give a thoughtful response.

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:03.027
Why are we talking like this?

00:07:03.027 --> 00:07:05.300
And it can lead to a conversation.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:06.593
Parents unite!

00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:10.920
But this morning was
more difficult than usual

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:15.590
because as you know, I
recently got solar panels

00:07:15.590 --> 00:07:19.810
put on the top of my house so
that I can go totally solar,

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.850
go off the grid, disconnect,

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:24.910
which is technically
illegal, I've learned.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.480
You can't disconnect from the
grid so I'm still on the grid

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:30.380
but now, I'm giving power
back to the power company.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.040
I don't know, solar panels are weird

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.596
but I'm glad we have them
but today was the day

00:07:34.596 --> 00:07:37.320
that they were gonna be turned on

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:40.060
after they pass inspection.

00:07:40.060 --> 00:07:41.100
Sounds like a big moment

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:43.130
where nothing perceptible happens.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.233
Right, especially when it's cloudy.

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:50.980
But my wife calls me last
night and says, "Oh."

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:52.920
You can call her Jessie.

00:07:52.920 --> 00:07:53.753
She has a name.

00:07:53.753 --> 00:07:54.980
No, I can't, she said I couldn't.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.510
Okay.

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:57.343
Just kidding.

00:07:57.343 --> 00:07:59.043
Jessie called me and said,

00:08:00.007 --> 00:08:03.527
"Oh, they're gonna cut the
solar panels on tomorrow

00:08:03.527 --> 00:08:04.967
"and the inspectors gonna be there

00:08:04.967 --> 00:08:07.497
"and the guy from the
solar company told me

00:08:07.497 --> 00:08:10.197
"that occasionally, the inspector will ask

00:08:10.197 --> 00:08:15.197
"to go inside the house to
inspect your smoke detectors."

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:18.467
Okay.

00:08:18.467 --> 00:08:20.020
I was like, okay, well.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.190
I went on the Internet.

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:25.180
I looked up like California regulations

00:08:25.180 --> 00:08:26.480
about smoke detectors.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.130
I assumed just based on what I've learned

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:29.210
about living in California

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:32.410
that they require like a
smoke detector in every room,

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:35.280
a smoke detector tied around
the neck of every person,

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.790
two smoke detectors on your pets,

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:40.360
one smoke detector on the
aquarium for your fish

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:43.300
and seven smoke detectors
on top of your house.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:44.133
And every smoke detector--

00:08:44.133 --> 00:08:45.140
There's a lot of regulations.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.190
Needs to have a sticker that says--

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.130
This can cause cancer.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.280
This smoke detector contains materials,

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:54.060
which are known to the state of California

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.850
to contain carcinogens.

00:08:55.850 --> 00:08:56.980
So not only it detects smoke--

00:08:56.980 --> 00:08:58.250
By the way.
But kill you.

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:00.300
Everything in California
has this sticker on it.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.090
You walk into a building
there's like a plaque.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.820
You open the door to your car,

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:06.820
there's a sticker on your car that says,

00:09:07.750 --> 00:09:10.760
anywhere you look, this so-and-so

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:14.170
has materials known to
the state of California

00:09:14.170 --> 00:09:15.200
to cause cancer.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.107
Yeah, so anyway, I was like,

00:09:17.107 --> 00:09:18.627
"Well, I don't know if we
got enough smoke detectors.

00:09:18.627 --> 00:09:19.907
"We got one in all the bedrooms

00:09:19.907 --> 00:09:22.258
"but like since we like redid the kitchen,

00:09:22.258 --> 00:09:25.000
"I think we got rid of some downstairs."

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:25.833
So,

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:29.313
I installed,

00:09:30.330 --> 00:09:32.870
first of all, smoke
detector's technology--

00:09:32.870 --> 00:09:33.703
Oh, gosh, yes.

00:09:33.703 --> 00:09:37.110
It's updated since last
time I looked into it.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.983
No longer, they don't come with a battery.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.640
Or they come with a
special kind of battery

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:47.520
that never requires you
to change it for years

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:49.110
and you don't have to put
regular batteries in there.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.160
You just take the thing
out and activate it.

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:54.210
Like press a button, flip a little switch

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.270
and then it's good to go.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:10:00.120
So, of course, we had dinner
last night with a friend

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:03.200
so we were out late and so I
didn't install it last night.

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:04.667
I was like, "I'll do it in the morning

00:10:04.667 --> 00:10:05.920
"before I take the kids to school,"

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:10.500
and then I kinda got up
late, they kinda got up late

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:12.380
and we're like struggling
to get out the door

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:13.827
and then I'm like, "Guys, sorry,

00:10:13.827 --> 00:10:16.247
"I got to install the
smoke detector right now."

00:10:17.278 --> 00:10:19.170
And so I'm like going
downstairs and getting the drill

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:22.060
so I could like drill 'cause
you got to do the little

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:25.400
drywall screws and that kind of thing.

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:27.517
And I'm like, "You're both gonna be

00:10:27.517 --> 00:10:28.900
"a little bit late for school."

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:29.913
That's the first thing I said.

00:10:29.913 --> 00:10:32.090
I was just setting expectations.

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:33.850
It's like, Dad has already failed.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:34.683
Yeah.

00:10:34.683 --> 00:10:36.970
But we got to have a smoke detector

00:10:36.970 --> 00:10:39.810
so I do the smoke detector,
did a pretty good job,

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.703
put it up there and activated it.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:45.560
We're already a little bit late
and then while I'm doing it,

00:10:45.560 --> 00:10:47.060
I'm being very dad-ish.

00:10:47.060 --> 00:10:48.477
I'm saying things like, "Is there anything

00:10:48.477 --> 00:10:50.290
"that you guys could be doing?"

00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:51.830
Yeah.

00:10:51.830 --> 00:10:54.180
I was like, "Locke,
check the freaking trash.

00:10:56.687 --> 00:10:57.537
"How's the trash?

00:10:57.537 --> 00:10:58.860
"Does the trash need to be taken out?"

00:10:58.860 --> 00:11:01.830
And he goes and he's like, "No."

00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:06.180
So then I had to throw away
the smoke detector packaging

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.013
and I go to the trash.

00:11:07.013 --> 00:11:09.200
The trash in the kitchen
is completely full.

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.810
And I'm like, "You lied to me!"

00:11:11.810 --> 00:11:12.780
Oh.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:13.960
I was like, "You said it was fine."

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:14.793
He was like, "It is fine."

00:11:14.793 --> 00:11:18.610
I was like, "Are we seeing
the same trash can right now?"

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:19.584
