00:47:52.670 --> 00:47:57.570
But yeah, it's more that
she what's the better person

00:47:57.570 --> 00:48:01.336
but you extended an olive branch.

00:48:01.336 --> 00:48:04.770
And she graciously accepted it,

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:08.150
'cause it's like even when you apologize,

00:48:08.150 --> 00:48:11.810
you feel like this is
gonna be picked apart.

00:48:11.810 --> 00:48:15.287
And I don't wanna get on a
soapbox here but like there's--

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:18.050
That's what happens in public.

00:48:18.050 --> 00:48:20.350
I'll inch on to the soapbox

00:48:20.350 --> 00:48:24.070
that like if somebody
sincerely apologizes,

00:48:24.070 --> 00:48:27.620
it's like, I know what
it feels like to be sorry

00:48:27.620 --> 00:48:32.160
and then not know exactly the
right words to say, you know?

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:36.860
And it feels good when you sincerely

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:40.600
empathize with pain you've caused

00:48:40.600 --> 00:48:44.750
and you're sorry for it
and then somebody has a,

00:48:44.750 --> 00:48:46.220
they have a gracious response

00:48:46.220 --> 00:48:50.275
because I think because
of the culture we're in,

00:48:50.275 --> 00:48:53.470
I had a heightened anxiety.

00:48:53.470 --> 00:48:58.460
And at first, that was part
of my knee-jerk response

00:48:58.460 --> 00:49:02.570
to say hi, you know what, we
can set the Facebook thing

00:49:02.570 --> 00:49:04.830
so she can't even see that we read this

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:07.890
so there's culpability that like okay,

00:49:07.890 --> 00:49:10.090
she doesn't even have
to know that we saw it

00:49:10.090 --> 00:49:11.460
or that I saw it.

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:14.550
And so, a reasonable conclusion

00:49:14.550 --> 00:49:15.460
could be that we didn't see it,

00:49:15.460 --> 00:49:17.610
not that I actively didn't respond

00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:19.980
because responding opens you up

00:49:19.980 --> 00:49:22.610
to a whole new level of criticism.

00:49:22.610 --> 00:49:23.804
And to be clear,

00:49:23.804 --> 00:49:28.804
we don't respond to every
complaint and every concern

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:32.830
because we get a lot of
them on a regular basis

00:49:32.830 --> 00:49:35.491
for all kinds of things that
people have been offended by.

00:49:35.491 --> 00:49:37.640
Yep, that's true.

00:49:37.640 --> 00:49:39.820
And sometimes, it feels like,

00:49:39.820 --> 00:49:41.922
but this felt different

00:49:41.922 --> 00:49:43.280
for reasons that you've already kinda--

00:49:43.280 --> 00:49:47.012
It was it was very deeply
personal and I just couldn't,

00:49:47.012 --> 00:49:48.750
I'm not tooting my own horn,

00:49:48.750 --> 00:49:51.490
I'm saying that like, I thought
about not saying anything.

00:49:51.490 --> 00:49:54.800
Yeah, I could and so I went
forward and then it was,

00:49:54.800 --> 00:49:57.242
but to me, it's like okay,

00:49:57.242 --> 00:50:00.140
what have I learned in this process?

00:50:00.140 --> 00:50:01.480
Well, before you move on 'cause I--

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:02.313
I think it's my last question but--

00:50:02.313 --> 00:50:04.680
I think you're making a really good point

00:50:04.680 --> 00:50:06.553
about the apology thing because,

00:50:08.660 --> 00:50:11.800
man, I've seen it so many
times in the past few years.

00:50:11.800 --> 00:50:13.250
Somebody does something

00:50:13.250 --> 00:50:16.053
that's legitimately upsetting to people.

00:50:18.319 --> 00:50:19.152
And a lot of times,

00:50:19.152 --> 00:50:21.090
it might be something
that was unintentional

00:50:21.090 --> 00:50:23.940
or just sort of misguided

00:50:23.940 --> 00:50:27.080
and then they apologize,

00:50:27.080 --> 00:50:29.450
they make an apology video on YouTube,

00:50:29.450 --> 00:50:32.350
they do the apology tweet where
you just take your notes app

00:50:32.350 --> 00:50:34.228
and you write something and
you put it on your Twitter,

00:50:34.228 --> 00:50:37.060
this is just a part of
culture at this point.

00:50:37.060 --> 00:50:38.470
People who have any sort of following

00:50:38.470 --> 00:50:41.113
or are public figures in any way,

00:50:41.980 --> 00:50:44.023
at times, will probably have to apologize

00:50:44.023 --> 00:50:47.190
and it is a guarantee

00:50:48.110 --> 00:50:51.300
that a certain percentage of the audience

00:50:51.300 --> 00:50:54.780
will see that the apology is inadequate,

00:50:54.780 --> 00:50:57.280
that they will get and try to figure out

00:50:57.280 --> 00:50:59.090
people's motivations for the apology.

00:50:59.090 --> 00:51:01.880
Oh, you're trying to protect
this but this is what you think

00:51:01.880 --> 00:51:05.240
and if you really were sorry,
this is what you would say

00:51:05.240 --> 00:51:07.810
and it's just like ,it
is just a part of culture

00:51:07.810 --> 00:51:10.830
to have somebody do
something and then apologize

00:51:10.830 --> 00:51:14.440
and then be jumped on for
apologizing not in the right way.

00:51:14.440 --> 00:51:18.220
It makes it really difficult
to even want to apologize.

00:51:18.220 --> 00:51:20.721
Now, there is a legitimate
assessment of an apology.

00:51:20.721 --> 00:51:21.554
Yeah.

00:51:21.554 --> 00:51:24.440
It's like we're not
saying that you shouldn't

00:51:24.440 --> 00:51:28.340
assess someone's apology to
see if it's a real apology

00:51:28.340 --> 00:51:33.340
and that if it's apologizing
for the actual infraction.

00:51:34.660 --> 00:51:35.810
Right.
You know, so.

00:51:35.810 --> 00:51:39.970
And we haven't had to do that personally.

00:51:39.970 --> 00:51:42.210
I guess I'm just saying that when I,

00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:46.470
because we are public
figures who invariably

00:51:46.470 --> 00:51:48.558
or inevitably will offend,

00:51:48.558 --> 00:51:51.710
already have and will continue
to, accidentally or whatever,

00:51:51.710 --> 00:51:54.860
offend people and then at some point,

00:51:54.860 --> 00:51:57.020
that offense may reach a threshold

00:51:57.020 --> 00:51:59.020
where we feel like we have to apologize.

00:52:00.020 --> 00:52:03.760
When I see it happening
because I can relate to just,

00:52:03.760 --> 00:52:05.270
oh, the anguish that someone is,

00:52:05.270 --> 00:52:07.147
ah, I shouldn't have said that
or I should have done that

00:52:07.147 --> 00:52:09.520
and now, I got to figure out
how to craft this in a way

00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:11.650
that will make it seem,

00:52:11.650 --> 00:52:16.280
it's just I have sympathy
for people in that position

00:52:16.280 --> 00:52:17.410
but I'm not saying that people

00:52:17.410 --> 00:52:19.370
should be able to do what they
want to and then apologize.

00:52:19.370 --> 00:52:23.500
I'm just saying that the
dynamic of apologizing sincerely

00:52:23.500 --> 00:52:25.430
and being accused of it being insincere

00:52:25.430 --> 00:52:26.683
is just something that,

00:52:27.970 --> 00:52:30.840
it's just a troublesome
part of this the way

00:52:30.840 --> 00:52:31.980
our culture works right now.

00:52:31.980 --> 00:52:36.980
In my house, I hope that
we set up an expectation

00:52:37.740 --> 00:52:42.067
of having to apologize

00:52:42.067 --> 00:52:46.523
and then a practice of
forgiving each other.

00:52:47.490 --> 00:52:49.810
I think that is a good practice

00:52:49.810 --> 00:52:53.380
because demanding that
you never do anything

00:52:53.380 --> 00:52:58.380
worth apologizing for or then
apologies become admitting

00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:01.230
not just what you did wrong,

00:53:01.230 --> 00:53:04.633
but like that you're a bad person.

00:53:07.040 --> 00:53:10.710
We all do things that
we need to apologize for

00:53:10.710 --> 00:53:14.180
so it shouldn't be a double
failure when you apologize.

00:53:14.180 --> 00:53:17.973
It should actually be a victory.

00:53:18.946 --> 00:53:22.670
A true apology should be a
victory, not a second defeat.

00:53:22.670 --> 00:53:23.730
Yeah.

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:27.213
So, what are my takeaways
from this experience?

00:53:30.381 --> 00:53:35.320
I'm gonna really think twice
before I use real names.

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:37.873
